.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-catalog-rebuild 1  "tapectl-catalog-rebuild " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-catalog\-rebuild \- Reconstruct catalog rows by reading a sealed volume
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl catalog rebuild\fR <\fB\-\-from\-volume\fR> [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] <\fB\-\-key\fR> [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-label\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-tenant\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reconstruct catalog rows by reading a sealed volume.
.PP
The path back when the database is gone and there is no backup.
.PP
Exit status: 0 = done; 2 = an error or a refusal; 75 = the catalog was busy.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-volume\fR
Rebuild from a sealed tape volume.

Required: the only source today, named rather than implied so a later `\-\-from\-export` is additive
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
Tape device (by\-id path).

Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured.

There is still no `/dev/nst0` fallback (ADR\-0010): `/dev/nstN` numbering is not stable across reboots on a host with more than one drive, and unlike a read\-only dump this command writes what it reads into the catalog — a silent wrong\-device default would file one tape\*(Aqs contents under another\*(Aqs. The sole configured drive\*(Aqs `device_tape` is not a guess about which drive you meant; a guessed device number is. Resolve by serial through `/dev/tape/by\-id/`.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Operator or escrow secret key file (an age identity); repeat for several.

Operator or escrow secret key file (an age identity, `AGE\-SECRET\-KEY\-1…`); repeat for several. For this command only: never copied into the home or registered as any key, so an heir can rebuild with the escrow key alone and then restore with the same `\-\-key`. A tenant key cannot open the operator envelope and is refused with a pointer at RESTORE.sh; a file that is not an identity is refused, naming it, before the drive is opened
.TP
\fB\-\-label\fR \fI<LABEL>\fR
Refuse unless the tape\*(Aqs own label matches (a wrong\-tape guard).

Refuse unless the tape\*(Aqs own reported label matches — a wrong\-tape guard, not a database lookup
.TP
\fB\-\-tenant\fR \fI<TENANT>\fR [default: recovered]
Tenant to file units under when no tenant envelope names their owner.

Tenant to file units under when no tenant envelope on this cartridge names their owner (only reachable with a damaged or partial tape)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
